    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "I'm looking for some help with my Monstera deliciosa. It's been sitting in my living room for a year and doing great, but lately, the lower leaves are starting to turn a pale yellow color. The soil feels kind of damp even when I think I've watered it, but I don't see any obvious pests. Any idea what might be going on?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Yellowing lower leaves on a Monstera, especially when the soil feels perpetually damp, often points to overwatering. This is a common issue, as damp soil can lead to root rot, which is a slow killer for these beautiful plants. You might need to let the soil dry out more thoroughly between waterings. Try sticking your finger a few inches down to check the actual moisture level before you water again.",
      "source": "target",
